IMPORTANT: AGPL §13 — REMOTE NETWORK INTERACTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The AGPL differs from the GPL in one respect that matters a great deal for
a protocol like Swarmbly, which is expected to be run as network-facing
infrastructure (relays, node runners, hosted orchestrators, gateways,
"Swarmbly as a service" offerings).

Section 13 of the AGPL-3.0 states, in relevant part:

    "Notwithstanding any other provision of this License, if you modify the
    Program, your modified version must prominently offer all users
    interacting with it remotely through a computer network (if your version
    supports such interaction) an opportunity to receive the Corresponding
    Source of your version by providing access to the Corresponding Source
    from a network server at no charge, through some standard or customary
    means of facilitating copying of software."

Practical consequences for anyone deploying Swarmbly:

  1. If you run a MODIFIED version of Swarmbly and users interact with it
     over a network, you must offer those users the Corresponding Source of
     your modified version, at no charge — even if you never distribute a
     binary or a copy of the code to anyone.

  2. "Users interacting with it remotely" includes users of a hosted or
     SaaS deployment. Running the software only on your own servers does
     not, by itself, avoid this obligation.

  3. The obligation attaches to the *modified* version. Running an
     unmodified upstream release over a network does not trigger §13's
     source-offer requirement, though offering the source anyway is good
     practice and costs nothing.

  4. The Corresponding Source must be complete enough to build and run the
     modified version — configuration and build scripts included.

